  <article class="article">
    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Maintenance</span>
    <h1>Motorbike Lights: A Pre-Ride Checklist for Hanoi Riders</h1>
    <p>Lights are a rider&rsquo;s voice in traffic. They announce intentions to vehicles that cannot see a face, they make a small machine visible in a dense one, and in Hanoi they do this work in dust, drizzle, and dusk. The pre-ride lights checklist is a sixty-second walk-around that confirms the voice is working before the day depends on it, and it is entirely within a rider&rsquo;s toolkit: hands on the controls, eyes on the results.</p>
    <p>It belongs to the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> hub, which collects the practical habits behind riding in and around the city. For where those habits can take you, see the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-trips/">Hanoi motorbike trips</a> hub, and for machines and rental basics, start at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> page.</p>
    <nav class="toc"><ol><li><a href="#sec-1">Why the Lights Minute Matters</a></li><li><a href="#sec-2">The Walk-Around: Headlight and Beam</a></li><li><a href="#sec-3">Brake Lights: The Signal Behind You</a></li><li><a href="#sec-4">Indicators, Horn, and the Complete Signal Set</a></li><li><a href="#sec-5">Rental Riders, Conditions, and the Honest Close</a></li></ol></nav>
    <h2 id="sec-1">Why the Lights Minute Matters</h2><p>A machine with a dead headlight is not merely inconvenient; it is statistically invisible. Drivers judge distance and speed from what they can see, and a scooter without working lights disappears in dusk and rain long before it disappears in fact. The lights minute exists because the failure mode is quiet: nobody warns you that a brake-light bulb died yesterday.</p><p>The check is unusually honest because lights are binary. A headlight is bright or it is not, a brake light works at the lever or it does not, and indicators flash at their usual rate or they do not. No experience, tools, or judgment calls are required, which makes this the most beginner-friendly item in the whole pre-ride ritual.</p><p>Hanoi&rsquo;s conditions add weight to the minute. Dusk arrives quickly at this latitude, drizzle blurs every windshield, and a large share of city riding happens within a few meters of vehicles whose drivers are watching everything at once. Working lights are the rider&rsquo;s claim on attention in that crowd, and the claim is worth sixty seconds of checking.</p><p>Rental machines deserve a special note. Bulbs and switches fail on their own schedule, and a machine that has stood between riders arrives with its history hidden. The lights check at pickup, run before accepting the machine, documents the working set while the desk can still act, and it takes the same sixty seconds the daily check takes.</p><p>The scope rule, stated once and honestly: the rider tests and reports. Replacing bulbs, adjusting switches, and chasing electrical faults belong to the shop or the desk. The rider&rsquo;s job is to know which lights should work, to confirm they do, and to stop riding the ones that do not, and that job is both safe and complete.</p>
    <h2 id="sec-2">The Walk-Around: Headlight and Beam</h2><p>Start at the front. Turn the key, confirm the dash is bright, and switch the headlight through low and high beam. Both should be clearly visible against a wall or in reflection, and the change between them should be obvious. One dead beam is a common and cheap fault, and it is exactly the kind of fault that survives unnoticed for weeks without a walk-around.</p><p>Check the beam direction while you are there. The pool of light should land on the road ahead, not in the eyes of oncoming drivers. A beam aimed high is not a rider-fixable item on most machines, but it is a desk-or-shop item worth mentioning, because in traffic a badly aimed headlight is a courtesy problem and a safety problem at the same time.</p><p>The lens deserves two seconds. Cracked, hazed, or water-fogged lenses cut light before it ever reaches the road, and condensation inside a lens often announces a seal that has given up. None of this is curb-repairable; all of it is worth a one-sentence report. A light that looks weak in the daytime is a light that will look absent at night.</p><p>Daytime running lights, where fitted, get the same glance. Many modern scooters run a permanent low light, and its absence is a genuine finding, not a cosmetic one. The pattern to remember: one dead component is usually that component; general dimness across everything points at the battery and charging story, which the battery guides on this hub cover in detail.</p><p>The headlight summary: bright, correctly aimed, clean, and complete, confirmed in sixty seconds against a wall. If the front of the machine reads any other way, ride only in daylight if at all, and report it. The rest of the checklist tells you whether the machine is safe for dusk, and dusk is when the front of the machine becomes the whole conversation.</p>
    <h2 id="sec-3">Brake Lights: The Signal Behind You</h2><p>The brake light is the light riders never see while using it, which is exactly why it needs a deliberate check. Stand beside the machine facing the back, pull the front brake lever, and watch. Then press the rear brake pedal and watch again. The light should snap on promptly at each control, because the two switches are independent and each can fail alone.</p><p>The rear brake check matters most in traffic, because the pedal is the brake a following driver reads at every slowdown. A dead pedal switch turns every stop into a surprise for the vehicle behind, and surprises in dense traffic are measured in dents. Two seconds of watching while working the pedal prevents the whole category.</p><p>Note the resting state as well. A brake light that stays on permanently, a fault riders sometimes discover from the behavior of cars behind them, confuses following drivers until they ignore it, which is worse than no light at all. The stuck-on switch is a desk-or-shop fix, and a machine with one is worth reporting before riding into rush hour.</p><p>Wiring and socket faults show up as flicker or intermittent operation: the light works, then does not, then does. Intermittent anything deserves more respect than a clean failure, because intermittent faults select the worst possible moment to become permanent. Flicker gets reported the same day, not ridden on for a week in hope.</p><p>The brake-light summary: prompt, clean operation at both controls, checked from behind the machine in five seconds total. It is the single highest-value item in the lights minute for city riding, and it is also the one most often skipped, because the rider cannot see the light from the riding position. Walk around once; the habit repays itself for the life of the machine.</p>
    <h2 id="sec-4">Indicators, Horn, and the Complete Signal Set</h2><p>Indicators get a rate check rather than an on-off check. Flash, pause, flash at the usual rhythm is healthy; a visibly lazier rhythm usually reports low battery voltage or a connection issue before anything else fails, and one dead side reports a bulb. The baseline habit matters here: riders who know their machine&rsquo;s rhythm hear the change within one ride.</p><p>Confirm both sides, front and rear. A dead rear indicator is invisible from the riding position and is precisely the one a following driver needed. The check is a fingertip on the switch and a glance over each shoulder, or a walk-around that catches both ends at once. Cancel the indicators afterward; a machine driving with one blinking is a machine telling the street a lie.</p><p>The horn closes the signal set. In Hanoi traffic the horn is a safety system, not a courtesy, and a weak, strangled horn is a battery&rsquo;s whisper before it is an inconvenience. Press it once at the curb each morning. The neighbors forgive a short blast at a reasonable hour, and the traffic may one day thank you for a working one.</p><p>The plate light, where fitted, is the lights minute&rsquo;s quiet last item. It costs a glance in dim light and exists because riding with an unreadable plate invites conversation with the authorities. The rider-level response is the same as every other item: notice, report, and let the desk or shop own the replacement.</p><p>The signal-set summary: indicators at their baseline rate on both sides, a horn with its full voice, a readable plate. Twenty seconds of checking, and the machine can announce slowing, turning, and presence to every vehicle that matters. A machine that cannot announce those things is a machine riding incognito, and incognito is the wrong disguise for two wheels.</p>
    <h2 id="sec-5">Rental Riders, Conditions, and the Honest Close</h2><p>Renters get the compressed version: run the full lights minute at pickup, before money and paperwork make it awkward. A working set at handover is the desk&rsquo;s promise, and confirming it turns later failures into the desk&rsquo;s problem at a fair price rather than a debate. Photographing the walk-around, as this hub&rsquo;s pickup guides suggest, documents the working set for both sides.</p><p>During the rental, the lights minute still runs daily but reports differently: to the desk, immediately, by message if that is the arrangement. Bulbs fail without malice on rented machines too, and a same-day report is a swap or a quick fix at the shop. Riding for days on a dead brake light converts a two-minute bulb into a liability conversation nobody enjoys.</p><p>Conditions raise the stakes, not the checklist. In steady rain or deep dusk, the same sixty seconds buys more safety, because lights do proportionally more work when visibility drops. The wet-season guides on this hub cover the riding habits that follow; the lights minute is the first half of that partnership and the cheapest half by far.</p><p>The honest boundary, repeated because it matters: none of this guide asks a rider to fix anything. Bulb replacement, switch adjustment, wiring, and aiming are shop and desk work, with machine-specific details and, on rentals, agreement implications. The rider&rsquo;s entire job is sixty seconds of observation and a one-sentence report, and that job, done daily, is what keeps everyone&rsquo;s costs down.</p><p>The closing summary: headlight bright and aimed, brake light prompt at both controls, indicators at baseline on both sides, horn at full voice, plate readable. Sixty seconds, once a day, once at pickup. The lights minute is the least skilled and most honest check in riding, and in city traffic it is the rider&rsquo;s entire public relations department, working for free in every mile.</p>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry"><h3>How do I check motorbike lights before riding?</h3><p>Run a sixty-second walk-around: key on and confirm the dash is bright, check the headlight on low and high beam, watch the brake light while pulling the front lever and pressing the rear pedal, confirm both indicators flash at their usual rate, and press the horn once. Anything dead, flickering, or visibly slow gets reported before the day depends on it.</p></article>
    <article class="faq-entry"><h3>Why is my brake light staying on all the time?</h3><p>A permanently lit brake light usually means a stuck or misadjusted brake-light switch, most often the rear pedal switch. It is not rider-fixable on most machines, and it is genuinely unsafe in traffic, because following drivers eventually ignore a light that never changes. Stop riding and report it to the rental desk or a shop the same day.</p></article>
    <article class="faq-entry"><h3>What does a slow indicator flash rate mean?</h3><p>Indicators flashing noticeably slower than usual usually report low battery voltage or a connection issue, rather than a bulb fault. Check the rest of the electrical sweep, lights, dash, horn, and note whether lights brighten with revs. Report the pattern to the desk or a shop; on an owned machine, have the battery and charging system tested.</p></article>
    <article class="faq-entry"><h3>Can I ride with a dead headlight during the day?</h3><p>Daytime riding with a dead headlight is a calculated risk rather than a plan: the machine is less visible, and dusk has a way of arriving mid-ride. If you must ride, keep it short, stay off fast roads, and report the fault immediately. A dead headlight at night ends the ride; on a rental, call the desk for a swap or fix first.</p></article>
